(window.DOM_LAMLA=window.DOM_LAMLA||{})["circulation-2"]={"lecture":"circulation-2","chapters":[{"slug":"local-regulation","must":[{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Autoregulation</strong> keeps tissue blood flow relatively constant when perfusion pressure changes because local arterioles adjust their resistance."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Arteriolar radius</strong> strongly controls blood flow because vascular resistance varies inversely with the fourth power of radius."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Autoregulation limits</strong> matter because maximally dilated arterioles cannot compensate further when perfusion pressure becomes very low."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Myogenic control</strong> responds to increased vessel-wall stretch by contracting vascular smooth muscle → increased resistance limits the pressure-driven rise in flow."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Reduced transmural pressure</strong> decreases arteriolar stretch → smooth muscle relaxes → resistance falls and flow is supported."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Transmural pressure</strong> is pressure inside a vessel minus surrounding tissue pressure; it determines the distending force on the wall."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Laplace's law</strong> for a cylinder states T = P × r → larger radius requires greater wall tension at the same transmural pressure."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>A capillary's tiny radius</strong> means it needs only a small wall tension, so a wall one cell thick can withstand blood pressure; a widened vessel (aneurysm) needs more tension and is more likely to burst."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Metabolic control</strong> increases blood flow when tissue demand exceeds supply because accumulated local substances dilate resistance vessels."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Low tissue oxygen</strong> makes the local arterioles dilate → more oxygenated blood reaches the under-supplied tissue."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>High CO₂, H⁺ and lactate</strong> accompany inadequate supply or increased metabolism → local arterioles dilate and tissue flow rises."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>K⁺, adenosine and ADP</strong> accumulate with cellular activity or ATP breakdown → they help dilate local vessels, especially in active myocardium."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Metabolite washout</strong> follows increased flow → local dilator signals fall and vascular resistance moves back towards resting levels."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Serotonin</strong> released from platelets at a vessel injury favours local vasoconstriction → blood flow through the injured region falls."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Cold and angiotensin II</strong> promote vasoconstriction → increased vascular resistance opposes blood flow."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Active hyperaemia</strong> occurs during increased tissue activity because accumulating metabolites dilate arterioles and raise blood flow to match demand."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong> occurs after temporary flow obstruction because metabolites accumulated during ischaemia and blood surges through dilated vessels on release."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Longer flow obstruction</strong> produces a greater or longer reactive hyperaemic response because more oxygen debt and vasodilator signals accumulate."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Endothelial nitric oxide</strong> is released with shear stress → it raises smooth-muscle cGMP and causes vasodilation."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Bradykinin, substance P and acetylcholine</strong> can stimulate endothelial nitric oxide release → underlying vascular smooth muscle relaxes."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Prostacyclin</strong> dilates vessels and inhibits platelet aggregation, whereas endothelin-1 constricts vascular smooth muscle."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Histamine (H₂) and ANP</strong> promote vasodilation; ANP can act directly on vascular smooth muscle."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Endothelial damage</strong> reduces nitric oxide availability → vessels lose some ability to dilate and become more prone to constriction."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Working skeletal muscle</strong> can receive more blood during exercise despite sympathetic activation because strong local metabolic dilation opposes constriction."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Brain, kidney and heart flow</strong> show strong autoregulation because stable perfusion is particularly important to their function."}],"numbers":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Value</th>\n  <th>Number</th>\n  <th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Resistance–radius relationship</td>\n  <td>R ∝ 1/r⁴</td>\n  <td>A small reduction in arteriolar radius sharply increases resistance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Resistance after radius is halved</td>\n  <td>16 times the original</td>\n  <td>Illustrates why small changes in vessel calibre strongly alter flow.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Cylindrical-vessel wall tension</td>\n  <td>T = P × r</td>\n  <td>At constant transmural pressure, larger vessels need greater wall tension.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","chains":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Myogenic autoregulation</strong>: Perfusion pressure rises → vessel wall stretches → smooth muscle contracts → arteriolar resistance rises → flow returns towards its previous level.</li>\n<li><strong>Metabolic autoregulation</strong>: Oxygen delivery falls → vasodilator metabolites accumulate → arterioles widen → flow increases → metabolites are washed away.</li>\n<li><strong>Active hyperaemia</strong>: Tissue activity increases → oxygen use and metabolite production rise → arterioles dilate → blood flow increases during activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong>: Blood flow is temporarily blocked → oxygen debt and metabolites accumulate → obstruction is removed → flow surges → accumulated metabolites clear.</li>\n</ol>\n","confuse":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Myogenic control</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Metabolic control</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main signal</td>\n  <td>Change in transmural pressure and wall stretch</td>\n  <td>Mismatch between tissue oxygen supply and demand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Response to rising pressure or inadequate supply</td>\n  <td>Stretch triggers smooth-muscle contraction</td>\n  <td>Metabolite accumulation triggers arteriolar dilation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Autoregulation</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Hyperaemia</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Aim</td>\n  <td>Keep blood flow relatively steady despite a pressure change</td>\n  <td>Increase blood flow when tissue needs or has been deprived of it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Typical trigger</td>\n  <td>Change in perfusion pressure</td>\n  <td>Increased activity or release of an obstruction</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Active hyperaemia</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Timing</td>\n  <td>While tissue activity is increased</td>\n  <td>After a temporary interruption of blood flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Cause</td>\n  <td>Activity-related vasodilator metabolites</td>\n  <td>Ischaemia-related metabolite accumulation and oxygen debt</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","traps":"<ul>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Autoregulation</strong> keeps flow constant at any blood pressure → Truth: It works within limits; fully dilated vessels cannot protect flow indefinitely.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Myogenic constriction</strong> occurs because metabolites accumulate → Truth: Myogenic contraction responds to wall stretch; metabolite accumulation favours dilation.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Reactive hyperaemia</strong> means increased flow during exercise → Truth: That is active hyperaemia; reactive hyperaemia follows temporary obstruction.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Laplace's law</strong> means larger vessels need less wall tension → Truth: At the same transmural pressure, tension rises with radius.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Nitric oxide</strong> constricts arterioles → Truth: Endothelial nitric oxide increases smooth-muscle cGMP and promotes dilation.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Sympathetic activation</strong> stops working-muscle vasodilation → Truth: Local metabolic signals can override constriction in active muscle.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"slug":"neural-humoral","must":[{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Mean arterial pressure</strong> depends mainly on cardiac output and total peripheral resistance → central control can change either to defend systemic pressure."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Cardiac output</strong> equals heart rate × stroke volume → changing rate, contractility or venous return can change cardiac output."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Central regulation</strong> coordinates the whole circulation, while local control adjusts blood supply to individual tissues."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Medullary pressor area</strong> in the upper lateral medulla excites spinal sympathetic neurons → vascular constriction and cardiac stimulation raise pressure."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Medullary depressor area</strong> in the lower medial medulla inhibits the pressor area → sympathetic outflow and pressure tend to decrease."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Nucleus tractus solitarius</strong> receives cardiovascular sensory input → medullary circuits adjust sympathetic and vagal output."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Sympathetic noradrenaline</strong> activates vascular α₁ receptors → arterioles and muscular veins constrict."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Resistance-vessel constriction</strong> narrows arterioles → total peripheral resistance rises and blood flow is redistributed."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Capacitance-vessel constriction</strong> reduces venous blood storage → venous return, ventricular filling and potential stroke volume increase."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Capillaries</strong> lack vascular smooth muscle → they are not directly constricted by sympathetic vasomotor nerves."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Tonic sympathetic discharge</strong> keeps arterioles partly constricted at rest → increasing or reducing this firing changes vessel calibre."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Reduced vasoconstrictor tone</strong> allows vessels to relax → vasodilation does not usually require activation of separate dilator fibres."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Sympathetic cholinergic dilator fibres</strong> can release acetylcholine to skeletal-muscle vessels, although their importance in humans is uncertain."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Cardiac sympathetic stimulation</strong> activates β₁ receptors → the heart beats faster, conducts impulses faster and contracts more forcefully."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Vagal stimulation</strong> activates cardiac M₂ receptors → sinoatrial firing and atrioventricular conduction slow, with little direct ventricular inotropic effect."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Resting vagal tone</strong> suppresses the sinoatrial node's intrinsic rate → the resting pulse is lower than the unrestrained pacemaker rate."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Vagotomy or atropine</strong> removes or blocks the vagal brake on the heart → resting heart rate rises."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Vagal control of pressure</strong> acts mainly through the heart because most systemic blood vessels have little parasympathetic innervation."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Adrenaline at β₁ receptors</strong> increases heart rate and contractility → cardiac output tends to rise."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Adrenaline at β₂ receptors</strong> dilates arterioles in skeletal muscle and liver → blood delivery to these beds can increase."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Adrenaline at α₁ receptors</strong> constricts vessels, particularly in skin and gut → blood is redirected away from those beds."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Angiotensin II</strong> constricts arterioles and promotes aldosterone, ADH and thirst → pressure and circulating volume are supported."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Aldosterone</strong> increases renal sodium retention → water follows and circulating volume is supported over hours to days."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>ADH (vasopressin)</strong> increases renal water retention and can constrict arterioles at high levels → circulating volume and pressure are supported."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Atrial natriuretic peptide</strong> is released by stretched atria → vasodilation and renal sodium/water loss help reduce volume overload."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Bradykinin</strong> promotes endothelial nitric oxide release → arterioles dilate; <strong>VIP</strong> also dilates vessels."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Rapid neural responses</strong> adjust pressure within seconds; circulating hormones reinforce the response over minutes, and kidneys regulate volume over longer periods."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Early haemorrhage compensation</strong> raises sympathetic firing and reduces vagal tone → faster beats, stronger contraction and vessel constriction defend pressure."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Blood-volume recovery</strong> requires retained or replaced salt and water → nerve-driven vasoconstriction alone cannot replace lost fluid."}],"numbers":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Value</th>\n  <th>Number</th>\n  <th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Unrestrained sinoatrial node rate</td>\n  <td>About 100 beats/min</td>\n  <td>Resting vagal tone normally slows the heart below this rate.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Resting heart rate</td>\n  <td>About 60–80 beats/min</td>\n  <td>The gap below 100 is the vagal brake (vagal tone).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Cardiac output relation</td>\n  <td>CO = HR × SV</td>\n  <td>Shows the two direct determinants of cardiac output.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Mean arterial pressure relation</td>\n  <td>MAP ≈ CO × TPR</td>\n  <td>Links cardiac pumping and arteriolar resistance to arterial pressure.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","chains":"<ol>\n<li><strong>Pressor response</strong>: Medullary pressor area becomes more active → spinal sympathetic discharge rises → arterioles and veins constrict → resistance and venous return rise → pressure increases.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac autonomic control</strong>: Sympathetic β₁ activation rises and vagal M₂ activation falls → heart rate and contraction strength rise → cardiac output tends to increase.</li>\n<li><strong>Adrenaline redistribution</strong>: Circulating adrenaline stimulates muscle β₂ receptors and skin/gut α₁ receptors → muscle vessels dilate while skin/gut vessels constrict → flow is redistributed.</li>\n<li><strong>Haemorrhage compensation</strong>: Blood volume falls → rapid sympathetic responses defend pressure → angiotensin II, ADH and aldosterone support retention → blood volume recovers as fluid becomes available.</li>\n</ol>\n","confuse":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Resistance vessels</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Capacitance vessels</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main vessels</td>\n  <td>Arterioles</td>\n  <td>Veins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Effect of sympathetic constriction</td>\n  <td>Increases resistance and redirects flow</td>\n  <td>Reduces blood storage and increases venous return</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Sympathetic cardiac control</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Vagal cardiac control</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main receptor</td>\n  <td>β₁</td>\n  <td>M₂</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Effect on rate</td>\n  <td>Increases sinoatrial firing</td>\n  <td>Decreases sinoatrial firing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Effect on ventricular force</td>\n  <td>Increases contractility</td>\n  <td>Little direct effect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Adrenaline at β₂</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Noradrenaline at α₁</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main vascular effect</td>\n  <td>Dilates responsive muscle vessels</td>\n  <td>Constricts arterioles and muscular veins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Consequence</td>\n  <td>Can increase local muscle flow</td>\n  <td>Raises resistance or mobilises venous blood</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Neural regulation</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Renal–hormonal regulation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Typical speed</td>\n  <td>Seconds</td>\n  <td>Minutes to days</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main strength</td>\n  <td>Rapid defence of pressure</td>\n  <td>Sustained control of sodium, water and volume</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","traps":"<ul>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Vasodilation</strong> requires firing of sympathetic dilator nerves → Truth: Most systemic dilation follows reduced tonic constrictor discharge.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Venoconstriction</strong> directly raises total peripheral resistance as its main effect → Truth: It mainly shifts stored blood towards the heart.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Vagotomy</strong> slows the heart → Truth: Removing resting vagal inhibition increases sinoatrial firing and heart rate.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Adrenaline</strong> has one effect on every vessel → Truth: β₂-mediated dilation and α₁-mediated constriction differ by vascular bed.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Parasympathetic nerves</strong> control most arterioles directly → Truth: Sympathetic tone dominates most systemic resistance vessels.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Rapid sympathetic compensation</strong> replaces lost blood → Truth: It supports pressure; sodium/water retention and fluid replacement restore volume.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"slug":"reflexes","must":[{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Cardiovascular reflexes</strong> link a stimulus, receptor, afferent nerve, medullary centre and autonomic output → they rapidly adjust the heart and vessels."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Arterial baroreceptors</strong> detect stretch of arterial walls rather than measuring pressure directly → greater stretch increases afferent firing."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Carotid sinus baroreceptors</strong> lie near the common carotid bifurcation → their afferents travel through the carotid sinus nerve and glossopharyngeal nerve IX."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Aortic arch baroreceptors</strong> monitor stretch in the aortic arch → their afferents travel mainly through vagus nerve X."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Nucleus tractus solitarius</strong> receives baroreceptor signals in the medulla → it alters pressor, depressor and vagal activity."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Rising arterial pressure</strong> increases baroreceptor firing → vagal activity rises and sympathetic activity falls, reducing cardiac output and vascular resistance."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Baroreflex cardiac slowing</strong> follows increased vagal output → heart rate falls; reduced sympathetic drive also lowers ventricular contractility."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Baroreflex vasodilation</strong> follows reduced sympathetic constrictor tone → arterioles relax and veins store more blood."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Falling arterial pressure</strong> reduces baroreceptor firing → sympathetic output rises and vagal output falls, causing tachycardia and vasoconstriction."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Venoconstriction during hypotension</strong> shifts blood towards the heart → venous return and stroke volume are supported."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Baroreflex negative feedback</strong> counters rapid pressure disturbances → it is especially useful during sudden standing or blood loss."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Chronic baroreceptor resetting</strong> shifts the firing response toward sustained pressure → the reflex cannot alone control long-term hypertension."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>A functioning baroreflex</strong> does not replace haemorrhaged blood → faster pulse and constriction can coexist with inadequate organ perfusion."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Low-pressure volume receptors</strong> in atria and pulmonary vessels detect filling-related stretch rather than high arterial pressure."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Type A atrial receptors</strong> fire during atrial systole and signal contraction; type B receptors fire during later filling and signal distension."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Increased atrial filling</strong> stimulates type B receptors → signals favour responses that reduce excess circulatory volume."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Atrial stretch</strong> can increase heart rate through the Bainbridge reflex → extra venous inflow is pumped onwards more quickly."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Bainbridge tachycardia</strong> is weak or variable in humans because arterial baroreceptor influences may outweigh the atrial response."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Atrial-volume responses</strong> can reduce ADH and ACTH release; reduced ADH allows greater renal water loss during volume overload."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Atrial natriuretic peptide</strong> increases with atrial stretch → renal sodium and water excretion increase, helping relieve volume overload."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Atrial-stretch vasodilation</strong> lowers peripheral resistance and may lower pressure even when heart rate increases."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Carotid and aortic bodies</strong> are peripheral chemoreceptors → they detect low oxygen, high carbon dioxide and increased H⁺."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Chemoreceptor activation</strong> primarily increases breathing and can increase sympathetic vasoconstriction when perfusion is dangerously low."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Direct medullary hypoxia or hypercapnia</strong> stimulates the vasomotor centre → sympathetic outflow and systemic arterial pressure rise."},{"star":true,"html":"<strong>Cushing reflex</strong> begins when raised intracranial pressure reduces medullary perfusion → sympathetic hypertension triggers secondary baroreflex bradycardia."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Cushing triad</strong> consists of hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing → it signals dangerous brainstem compromise."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Tumour, blood clot or hydrocephalus</strong> can raise intracranial pressure → reduced cerebral perfusion may trigger the Cushing response."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Anger, excitement and brief sharp pain</strong> can increase medullary sympathetic drive through higher centres → arterial pressure rises."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Fear, grief or prolonged severe pain</strong> can produce vasodilation and bradycardia → pressure falls and fainting may occur."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Lung inflation</strong> sends inhibitory vagal afferent signals to the vasomotor centre → vasodilation can lower blood pressure."},{"star":false,"html":"<strong>Opposite heart-rate patterns</strong> arise because haemorrhage decreases baroreceptor firing, whereas Cushing hypertension increases baroreceptor firing."}],"numbers":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Value</th>\n  <th>Number</th>\n  <th>Why it matters</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Carotid sinus sensory nerve</td>\n  <td>IX</td>\n  <td>Glossopharyngeal nerve carries carotid sinus pressure signals to the medulla.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Aortic arch sensory nerve</td>\n  <td>X</td>\n  <td>Vagus nerve carries aortic arch pressure signals to the medulla.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","chains":"<ol>\n<li><strong>High-pressure baroreflex</strong>: Blood pressure rises → carotid and aortic walls stretch → baroreceptor firing rises → vagal output increases and sympathetic output falls → heart rate and resistance decrease.</li>\n<li><strong>Low-pressure baroreflex</strong>: Blood pressure falls → arterial stretch and afferent firing fall → pressor output rises and vagal drive falls → heart and vessels increase pressure towards normal.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial volume reflex</strong>: Venous filling rises → atrial type B receptors discharge → ADH falls and ANP rises → sodium/water excretion increases; Bainbridge tachycardia may occur.</li>\n<li><strong>Cushing reflex</strong>: Intracranial pressure rises → medullary blood flow falls → local hypoxia and CO₂ rise → sympathetic hypertension develops → arterial baroreceptors trigger bradycardia.</li>\n</ol>\n","confuse":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Arterial baroreceptors</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Atrial volume receptors</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main location</td>\n  <td>Carotid sinus and aortic arch</td>\n  <td>Atria and pulmonary vessels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main stimulus</td>\n  <td>Arterial-wall stretch</td>\n  <td>Filling-related stretch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Key response</td>\n  <td>Buffers sudden arterial pressure change</td>\n  <td>Helps adjust excess circulating volume</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Carotid sinus</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Carotid body</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Receptor</td>\n  <td>Baroreceptor</td>\n  <td>Chemoreceptor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Detects</td>\n  <td>Arterial-wall stretch</td>\n  <td>Low O₂, high CO₂ and increased H⁺</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Baroreflex after haemorrhage</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Cushing reflex</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Initial problem</td>\n  <td>Arterial pressure falls</td>\n  <td>Intracranial pressure compromises medullary flow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Heart-rate response</td>\n  <td>Tachycardia from reduced baroreceptor firing</td>\n  <td>Bradycardia after sympathetically driven hypertension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th></th>\n  <th><strong>Type A atrial receptor</strong></th>\n  <th><strong>Type B atrial receptor</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Timing</td>\n  <td>During atrial contraction</td>\n  <td>During later atrial filling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Main information</td>\n  <td>Atrial contraction</td>\n  <td>Atrial filling and distension</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n","traps":"<ul>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Baroreceptors</strong> directly sense blood chemistry → Truth: They sense arterial stretch; carotid and aortic bodies sense blood gases and H⁺.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Higher blood pressure</strong> increases sympathetic output through the normal baroreflex → Truth: Increased stretch reduces sympathetic output and increases vagal activity.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>A rapid pulse after haemorrhage</strong> means perfusion is restored → Truth: Tachycardia is compensation; circulating volume may remain severely reduced.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Atrial stretch</strong> necessarily slows the heart → Truth: Bainbridge signalling can increase rate, although the effect is variable in humans.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Cushing bradycardia</strong> means sympathetic drive is low → Truth: Medullary ischaemia first raises sympathetic pressure; the arterial baroreflex then slows the heart.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Cushing hypertension</strong> guarantees adequate brain blood flow → Truth: It compensates for dangerous cerebral underperfusion caused by high intracranial pressure.</li>\n<li>Trap: <strong>Pain</strong> always raises blood pressure → Truth: Brief sharp pain raises it, but prolonged severe pain can lower it and cause fainting.</li>\n</ul>\n"}]};
